```apex
/**
 * Normalizes Contact.Phone values so they only contain digits and a leading "+",
 * which is all the new dialer accepts.
 *
 * Bulk-safe: one SOQL query and one Database.update(contacts, false) call,
 * with per-record error reporting instead of an all-or-nothing DML.
 */
public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    /** Anything that is not a digit or a plus sign. Compiled once, reused per record. */
    private static final Pattern NON_DIALER_CHARS = Pattern.compile('[^0-9+]');

    /**
     * Strips every character that is not a digit or '+' from the Phone of each
     * supplied Contact and saves them in a single partial-success update.
     *
     * @param contactIds Contacts to process (up to 200 per call).
     * @return Map of Contact Id -> first error message for every Contact that could
     *         not be saved. Empty when every Contact was saved successfully.
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
              AND Phone != null
        ];

        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            // Contacts without a phone (null or blank) are left out entirely.
            if (String.isBlank(c.Phone)) {
                continue;
            }
            c.Phone = NON_DIALER_CHARS.matcher(c.Phone).replaceAll('');
            toUpdate.add(c);
        }

        if (toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(toUpdate, false);

        // SaveResult order matches the order of the input list, so index i maps
        // back to toUpdate[i] and therefore to a specific Contact Id.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                Id contactId = toUpdate[i].Id;
                List<Database.Error> errors = sr.getErrors();
                String message = errors.isEmpty() ? 'Unknown error' : errors[0].getMessage();
                failures.put(contactId, message);
            }
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

Notes on what I changed and why:

- **Guards for `null`/empty input and an empty update list.** `Database.update` with an empty list is a no-op in practice, but returning early keeps the method safe for an empty or null `contactIds` and avoids a pointless DML call. The return type contract (empty map) is unchanged.
- **Skipped blank phones in Apex as well as in SOQL.** `Phone != null` does not exclude `''` or whitespace-only strings. A Contact whose phone is `''` "has no phone", so it should not be updated; I filter those out with `String.isBlank` before normalizing.
- **Precompiled `Pattern` instead of `String.replaceAll`.** `String.replaceAll` recompiles the regex on every call. At 200 records it hardly matters, but a static `Pattern` is the bulk-friendly habit and keeps the character class in one named place.
- **Used `with sharing`.** The default (no sharing keyword) is effectively `without sharing`, which silently ignores the running user's record access. This class is called from a dialer flow/UI context, so it should respect sharing unless you tell me otherwise.
- **Mapped failures by index.** `Database.update(..., false)` returns `SaveResult`s in the same order as the input list, so indexing into the list you actually passed to DML is the reliable way to get the Contact Id for each failure.
- **Assumption worth flagging:** if a phone contains *only* punctuation (e.g. `"( )"`), the normalized value is an empty string and the record is still updated to blank. That follows your rule literally ("remove every character that is not a digit or `+`"). If you'd rather leave such records untouched, add `if (String.isEmpty(normalized)) continue;` before adding to `toUpdate` — say the word and I'll adjust.
- **Assumption on ordering/leading `+`:** I did not enforce that the `+` must be leading or that only one is allowed; the requirement was only to remove disallowed characters, so `"+44 (20) 7946-0018"` becomes `"+442079460018"` exactly as specified.

